## 3. Throw handling

### Which rotation value is used — the **message**, not the live value ✅

**`RockLauncher.cs:69-104`** (`C:\Users\Ayden's mini\...\Assets\Scripts\Game\RockLauncher.cs`):

```csharp
private void OnThrow(ThrowMessage message)
{
    if (TurnManager.HasInstance)
    {
        var turns = TurnManager.Instance;
        if (turns.Phase != MatchPhase.Playing) return;
        if (message.playerId != turns.ActivePlayer) return;
    }
    if (_preview == null) { Debug.LogWarning("[RockLauncher] No AimPreview ..."); return; }
    if (_active != null && _active.IsFlying) return;   // line 89 — one rock at a time

    var tuning = _preview.Tuning;
    var stats  = RockStats.Default;

    var launch = SkipPhysics.CreateLaunch(
        _preview.ThrowOrigin.position, _preview.ThrowForward,
        message.power, message.timingQuality,
        message.aimDeg, message.attackAngleDeg, message.spinRate,
        stats, tuning);

    _active = CreateRockObject();
    _active.Launch(launch, stats, tuning, message.playerId);
}
```

Every value comes from the `ThrowMessage` itself — `message.aimDeg`, `message.attackAngleDeg`, `message.spinRate`, `message.power`, `message.timingQuality`. **No live `_curAim` is read.** There are **no coroutines, no animation delays, no `Invoke`, no `WaitForSeconds`** — the rock is created and launched synchronously in the same frame the message is dispatched. `AimPreview.OnThrow` (129-142) likewise snaps to the message's values.

**So Unity is not the source of the "stale throw".** The staleness is upstream, and it's a *perceptual* mismatch — see §6.

Rock integration, `RockController.cs:46-78`: runs in `Update` with its own 0.02 s accumulator and a 16-step guard, not `FixedUpdate`. Frame-rate independent, correct.

### Phone-side throw computation

**`controller/js/throw.js:163-199`**:

```js
export function computeThrowDescriptor(samples, tuning = ThrowTuning) {
  if (!samples || samples.length === 0) return null;
  const release = samples[samples.length - 1];              // newest sample
  const windowStart = release.t - tuning.swingWindowMs;     // 1500 ms
  let peak = null;
  for (const sample of samples) {
    if (sample.t < windowStart) continue;
    if (peak === null || sample.power > peak.power) peak = sample;
  }
  if (peak === null || peak.power < tuning.minValidPeakAccel) return null;   // 3 m/s²
  const releaseDelayMs = Math.abs(release.t - peak.t);
  return {
    power: normalize01(peak.power, tuning.minPeakAccel, tuning.maxPeakAccel),   // 6 → 35
    timingQuality: 1 - normalize01(releaseDelayMs, 0, tuning.peakWindowMs),     // 250 ms
    aimDeg: clamp(toGameYaw(release.dYaw), -tuning.maxAimDeg, tuning.maxAimDeg),// ±180
    attackAngleDeg: clamp(release.dBeta, tuning.minAttackDeg, tuning.maxAttackDeg), // ±60
    rollDeg: release.dGamma,
    spinRate: Math.hypot(release.rotationRate.alpha, release.rotationRate.beta, release.rotationRate.gamma),
    peakAccel: peak.power, releaseDelayMs, sampleCount: samples.length,
  };
}
```

The `release` sample is the newest ring-buffer entry (≤16 ms old at 60 Hz). Aim is taken live to the last frame — as documented. The release is bound to `touchend` in HOLD mode (`app.js:134-135`) / `touchstart` in TAP mode (`app.js:126-127`).

